@vsm 1.0
// docs/incidents/2026-08-10_boot_problems_catalog.vsm
// CATALOG, not a single incident — 22 distinct problems found across two
// audit passes on the SESSION BOOT PROCESS (primers, RULES.md, onboard.sh,
// hooks, MCPs, TASKS.vsm parsing), both inside one calendar day, 2026-08-10
// 16:12–22:20 -0400. Author: Claude [O] + rmw3 (S5), compiled 2026-08-11 at
// rmw3's request ("puedes listar todos los problemas... asociados al boot
// en los ultimos 3 dias?"). Measured, not the 3 days asked for — git log
// shows nothing boot-related on 2026-08-08 or 2026-08-09; everything below
// is one ~6h window. Reported as measured, not padded to match the ask.
//
// NAMING COLLISION, resolved here so it doesn't recur: several commits this
// same day contain "boot/" in their message (cb7bde25, 341234ab, 2f640917,
// 08007f6a) — that "boot/" is a docs/spec_revision/ module (axiom-citation
// tokenizer work on the VSL restructure), UNRELATED to the session boot
// process this catalog covers. Excluded below on purpose.
//
// Status of this file: DRAFT → amended. Section catalog_ds_addendum was
// filled by DS 2026-08-11 (dispatch docs/deploy/2026-08-11_ds_dispatch_
// boot_catalog_review.vsm) — addendum written from DS's own vantage, not [O]'s.

catalog("BOOT-PROBLEMS-2026-08-10") {

  scope: "Session bootstrap only: docs/spec/agent_protocol/ primers,
      RULES.md, scripts/state/onboard.sh, .claude/hooks/*, state-canon /
      state-canon-infra MCP wiring, TASKS.vsm parsing (both onboard.sh's
      inline parser and state-canon-mcp's tasks_provider.py). Does NOT cover
      the docs/spec_revision/boot/ module (see naming collision note above),
      infra/service incidents, or non-boot TASKS.vsm items.",

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // AUDIT 1 — 16:12–~19:00, session("2026-08-10-claude-boot-audit")
  // Source: HANDOFF.vsm.md session block (that name), read in full before
  // writing this catalog — not recalled.
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  audit_1: [
    "01. onboard.sh parsed only ONE of TASKS.vsm's three task() syntaxes —
        hid 17 open tasks including two P1s. Fixed: commits 35ae861c,
        3d849ebe ('onboard: 1347 lines -> 130, and it stops hiding things').",
    "02. onboard.sh section 5 always printed the OLDEST HANDOFF block,
        mislabeled 'Last'. Fixed same rewrite (3d849ebe).",
    "03. 84% of onboard.sh's output was one sev-0.0 algedonic line repeated
        387 times — volume, not signal. Fixed same rewrite.",
    "04. onboard.sh's four vsf-q wrapper calls swallowed stderr — a failed
        query rendered as an empty section, indistinguishable from
        'nothing to report'. Fixed same rewrite.",
    "05. Nothing in onboard.sh checked whether state.db was still being
        WRITTEN — a stale census would have read as current. Fixed same
        rewrite (freshness check added to §2 services).",
    "06. CLAUDE.md carried THREE parallel-authority tables, one truncated
        mid-sentence since June. Fixed: commit 56cae0b
        ('CLAUDE.md is an index again — 268 lines to 149').",
    "07. Hard rules (R5/R6/R7) lived in 3-4 independently drifting copies
        across RULES.md and the primer docs. Fixed: commit 1a4f5888
        ('boot audit L0-L1: the hard rules now have one definition, not four')
        — vsl_hard_rules_v1.vsm made normative, RULES.md cites it.",
    "08. Hook vsf-onboard-reminder did not fire on a clean `startup`, only
        on compact/resume — a cold session start got no VSF boot reminder
        at all. Fixed: commit ee67dd13
        ('the boot reminder now fires on clean starts, and says the right thing').",
    "09. HANDOFF.vsm.md (755 KB) and TASKS.vsm (1341 KB) had grown past any
        practical read limit. Fixed: commits 31734b4 ('HANDOFF split by
        month — 755 KB to 216 KB'), 3ffca7a ('TASKS.vsm 1341 KB -> 691 KB'),
        69993a0 ('archives move to docs/sessions').",
    "10. R15 (formal cross-agent exchange must be VSL) had NO enforcement —
        envelope.py existed, passed its own selftest, and no module
        imported it; it was also narrower than the canon (rejected
        multi-line values and the Unicode φ/→ form the primer itself
        writes — rejecting the real Qwen and Kimi exchanges on file).
        Fixed: commits 0a2b3b5 ('R15 has enforcement now'), 24e6cd3
        ('R15 gate: print the glyph, not its escape').",
    "11. R4 (a claim of success is not evidence of success) had no gate —
        enforced by intention alone. Fixed: commit 949b879 ('R4 gets a
        gate: a verification claim must name a check that discriminates') —
        scripts/vsl/verify_claim.py + checks/ + commit-msg hook.",
    "12. FIVE verification failures by [O] in that same session, same shape
        each time — asserted success from a PROXY for the claim rather than
        the claim itself: grep -qs with an empty pattern; git ls-files
        checked after a blocked commit; bc-less arithmetic that still
        printed a checkmark; a grep truncated past its own match; a hook's
        line count identical for valid and invalid input. Source: HANDOFF
        session('2026-08-10-claude-boot-audit').my_failures.",
    "13. WORST of the five: while 'proving' the new R15 gate blocked a bad
        envelope, the probe ran inside a worktree whose git index held a
        LIVE NATS TOKEN staged, and it got committed to main as f5dfb28.
        [O] reported the gate had blocked it. It had not. main was rolled
        back to 9e9ba52; the token is unreachable from every branch and
        absent from the working tree; the orphan object survives until
        `git gc --prune=now` (blocked for the agent — rmw3's to run,
        still not run as of this catalog). rmw3 ruled: no rotation needed,
        exposure was local (never pushed).",
    "14. A SECOND failure class, named by rmw3 at that session's close, not
        covered by the R4 gate: skipping documented procedure while
        otherwise enforcing it. Sent PROSE to DS after a full day spent
        building R15 enforcement. Filed a dispatch by old habit/precedent
        in docs/deploy/ instead of the receiving agent's own inbox, per
        what the protocol actually specifies. Invented a new
        docs/historical/ directory while docs/sessions/ and docs/INDEX.md
        already existed for exactly that purpose — then reported 'HANDOFF
        is missing most DS sessions' without opening either.",
  ],

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // AUDIT 2 — this conversation, ~19:xx–22:20, task(BOOT-RECONCILE-2026-08-10)
  // Every item below independently re-verified by [O] this session, not
  // taken from any report — see docs/spec/self_audit/boot_reconciliation.spec.vsm
  // for full derivation.
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  audit_2: [
    "15. state-canon MCP's tasks_provider.py silently dropped 2 real open
        tasks (DASHAI-S4-LAB-EVAL, VSF-FEED-SPEC-REVIEW) — its block
        collector counted '{'/'}' naively, so a literal '{' quoted inside a
        body string inflated depth and swallowed every block after it.
        Confirmed live: 351 (onboard.sh) vs 349 (state-canon) task ids.
        Fixed: state-canon-mcp commit 4fdb16a (_structural_brace_delta()).",
    "16. CLAUDE.md's hook table drifted AGAIN — said 'compact, resume' when
        .claude/settings.json already fired vsf-onboard-reminder on 4
        matchers (item 08 above added `startup`, but the table was never
        re-synced). Fixed: vsf commit 97da15d8 (P1).",
    "17. TWO opencode.json files existed with no reference to each other —
        /home/rmw3/vsf/opencode.json (root) and
        /home/rmw3/vsf/.opencode/opencode.json — each declaring different
        MCP servers, so DS's actual instrument wiring was silently split
        across both. Found live while drafting this catalog's own
        predecessor dispatch. Fixed: vsf commit 97da15d8 (P2b, merged to
        the single root file).",
    "18. Inside the duplicate config: a live-looking Google API key
        (mcp.stitch.headers.X-Goog-Api-Key) committed since 2026-07-22
        (commit 0bcb1fc), present in HEAD and 4 commits. Pulled from the
        working tree: vsf commit f80a63f1 (structure kept, value replaced
        with a pointer to task(STITCH-KEY-EXPOSURE-2026-08-10)).",
    "19. That key is CONFIRMED still live on GitHub right now — origin
        (git@github.com:rm-w3kufe/vsf.git) has been frozen in its
        PRE-2026-08-03-purge history since the NATS-token filter-repo run
        (item 13's remediation had this exact side effect, documented but
        the origin question left open — see task(GIT-HISTORY-REWRITE-2026-08-03)).
        `git fetch origin` + pickaxe search confirmed: origin/main =
        b1e2ffb, and it contains the key at commit 913932d2. rmw3
        deprioritized (repo is private) rather than resolve now.",
    "20. codebase-memory-mcp's global SessionStart hook asserts 'ALWAYS use
        codebase-memory-mcp FIRST for ANY code exploration' on every VSF
        boot, but /home/rmw3/vsf was never indexed (list_projects confirmed
        live) and CLAUDE.md's own instrument table never named the tool —
        two boot-time authorities disagreeing on what to reach for.
        Resolved: rmw3 picked option (B) with DS's kernel carve-out
        (projects/vsf-nucleus/src, on demand only) — implemented in
        CLAUDE.md's Instruments section, commit ce6c429f. Full tradeoff at
        docs/spec/self_audit/codebase_memory_scope_decision.vsm.",
    "21. A dormant-but-real indentation-anchoring divergence: DS found
        during P2 that tasks_provider.py's three block-recognition regexes
        are column-0-anchored while onboard.sh's are DELIBERATELY tolerant
        of leading whitespace (scripts/state/onboard.sh:95, 'TOLERATED on
        purpose'). 0 live indented task() defs existed at find-time, but the
        class was real and unguarded on state-canon's side — the same
        shape of bug as item 15, one level down. Fixed: state-canon-mcp
        commit 1520a9b; check re-documented in vsf commit abd17bb9;
        task closed in f5bbd5fc.",
    "22. [O]'s own round-2 dispatch predicted the P4 fix would make the
        indentation-divergence negative-control fixture fully converge.
        It did not — DS tested empirically and found PARTIAL convergence
        (id set agrees, open/partial set still diverges, because
        onboard.sh's form-B open-extraction regex stays column-0-anchored
        even though its id-extraction is tolerant). DS documented the true
        divergence vector and the designed alarm rather than either
        forcing full convergence (would have meant touching onboard.sh
        outside the dispatch's scope) or silently accepting the wrong
        prediction. A dispatcher's prediction is not canon either — this
        item exists so that asymmetry gets named, not just corrected.",
  ],

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // UNRESOLVED — as of this catalog, 2026-08-11
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  unresolved: [
    "task(STITCH-KEY-EXPOSURE-2026-08-10) — rmw3-only. Old key value still
        needs revoking at Google's console (pulling it from the file does
        not invalidate it). Git-history disposition parked by rmw3 ('git es
        privado, a la mierda, no me interesa verlo ahora') — not blocking
        anything else, but not closed either.",
    "task(GIT-HISTORY-REWRITE-2026-08-03) — rmw3-only, L3 (no technical
        blocker, no option reversible by merge). Entangled with the item
        above: same origin, same three dispositions (force-push / recreate
        / abandon origin). Open since 2026-08-03's NATS-token purge,
        i.e. BEFORE this catalog's own window — the oldest unresolved
        item this catalog touches.",
    "task(R7-SCOPE-INVERSION) — rmw3-only, L3 (hard-rule/canon scope
        change, A5 approval required). state.db's `rules` mirror still
        lacks R13/R15 and still carries 3 deploy-scope rows the 2026-08-03
        compression merged away — R7 as written makes the stale copy beat
        the corrected one.",
    "task(TASK-STATUS-VOCABULARY-NORMALISE) — steps 1-2 (census + proposal)
        DONE (session('2026-08-10-ds-status-vocab-steps1-2'), report-only).
        Step 3 (apply the mapping) gates on rmw3 ruling the ambiguous
        status set (6 distinct values + 3 same-family, listed in
        docs/deploy/2026-08-10_ds_dispatch_task_status_vocabulary.vsm §4).
        Step 4 (re-run tasks_archive.py --closed) waits on step 3.",
    "RESOLVED 2026-08-11 (rmw3 ran it, [O] verified). `git gc --prune=now`
        alone did NOT remove the object first try — it was still reachable
        via refs/heads/main@{1}'s reflog entry (the main rollback to
        9e9ba52 left f5dfb28 there, reflogs are a separate reachability
        root gc doesn't touch by default). Fix: `git reflog expire
        --expire=now refs/heads/main` (scoped to main only, not --all, to
        avoid discarding feat/ds-s1's reflog too) then `git gc --prune=now`
        again. Verified after: `git cat-file -e f5dfb28` -> 'fatal: Not a
        valid object name' (object gone, not just unreachable); `git
        reflog --all | grep f5dfb28` -> empty; `git fsck` -> clean, no
        output; HEAD/log/working-tree unaffected.",
  ],

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // DS ADDENDUM — filled by DS, not [O]. See dispatch envelope.
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  catalog_ds_addendum: "FILLED 2026-08-11 by DS (dispatch docs/deploy/2026-08-11_ds_dispatch_boot_catalog_review.vsm). Observations from DS's own vantage on the same day's work + readiness for all 5 unresolved — see ds_vantage[] and readiness[] below.",

  // ── DS ADDENDUM — my vantage on 2026-08-10's boot-adjacent work ─────────
  // Three work streams ran same-day that [O]'s catalog touches only in
  // passing; each left a boot-adjacent trace a Claude-Code session would
  // not have seen because the work happened on the state-canon/scripts side.

  ds_vantage: [
    "status-vocab census (TASK-STATUS-VOCABULARY-NORMALISE steps 1-2, report docs/deploy/2026-08-10_ds_task_status_vocabulary_census.vsm) measured 32 distinct status values, 44 unreadable blocks at 18:07; by 22:00 the same day [O]'s fixes (87948efd stub-gen repair, ecc456c6 form-C inline read + 2 invisible open tasks declared, 2b12e352 dedupe) had closed EVERY unreadable class — re-measured 2026-08-11 03:26: 355 task blocks · 355 readable · 0 unreadable. The vocabulary is still open (32 values, 8 proposed) — that is now step 3, waiting only on rmw3's ambiguous-set ruling; it is NOT a parser gap anymore.",
    "NATS filesystem sweep (commit c6d5178c, 2026-08-03, pre-catalog but in item-13's remediation history): scanned all 15 LXCs + host in place (stopped via pct mount), found 3 previously-unknown OLD-token locations (LXC101 /root/nucleus-wrapper.sh — the real env source for a unit with no Environment= lines; /etc/vsf/nats.token on 104+107; LXC112 triple: nats.token + /etc/environment + cascade_test.py). Boot-relevant beyond the secret itself: a unit can be 'active per systemd' while auth-muted for hours (kernel ran ~4.5h that way), so 'is-active' is NOT a token-health signal — the same R4 class item 13 describes, on the filesystem side. Registry V2 documents the 3 new location types for the next rotation.",
    "PH-09/PH-11 fuse work (commits afb60973/52fe2231, 9d9b08ca/2bab05fd/c1930071, 2026-08-09): system/fuse publishing — infra_ops.vsm and the spec/ moves. Boot-adjacent trace: none of these touch agent_protocol/ or RULES.md, so no boot doc moved. But the PH-11-cycle compression pattern (declared density gates, ledger ops) is the same declare-then-verify discipline the boot's own docs need — worth mirroring for TASKS.vsm's read-limit hygiene, not a boot fix.",
    "RE_STATUS trap surfaced in my PH-14 session (ledger op, 2026-08-10 01:30): scripts/vsl_parser.py:66 RE_STATUS is a line-scanning regex that trips on ANY status-style assignment ('at-status equals word') anywhere in a document, INCLUDING inside quoted prose — the ledger had been failing the grammar gate at HEAD unnoticed because its own validate block never claimed grammar. Boot-adjacent: the same regex class that hid task statuses (line-scan vs structure) exists one level up in the canon grammar path. Not touched by me (owner = scripts/ owner).",
  ],

  // ── READINESS — for each of the 5 unresolved[], what I would execute the
  // instant the rmw3 gate clears. The gate is the ONLY remaining blocker:
  // no design question is left to re-derive. Nothing below was executed —
  // 4 of 5 are rmw3-only by A5, and the 5th is gated on rmw3's ruling.

  readiness: [
    "STITCH-KEY-EXPOSURE-2026-08-10 (rmw3-only: rotate at Google + decide git-history): when the gate clears I would (1) verify the working tree carries only the pointer form (no AIza-prefixed value) in root opencode.json — it does, f80a63f — and re-check after any future edit; (2) if rmw3 brings a replacement key: write it into root opencode.json mcp.stitch.headers (one-line edit, structure already preserved), flip enabled:false → true, restart opencode, verify the 3 mcp entries load (state-canon-infra + state-canon-mcp must stay live — stitch must not shadow them); (3) if rmw3 decides git-history disposition (force-push / recreate / abandon origin): execute exactly that, then verify the key is absent from every reachable ref (pickaxe grep on all branches) and from origin/main.",
    "GIT-HISTORY-REWRITE-2026-08-03 (rmw3-only, L3): three dispositions, each mechanical once chosen. (a) abandon origin — nothing to run, just close the task and record the decision. (b) force-push a cleaned history — requires the filter-repo/filter-branch run first (I would prepare the command list, rmw3 runs it: the SAME operation that is STILL PARKED for the Stitch key, items 18/19 — do both histories in one pass if rmw3 approves). (c) recreate origin — clone fresh, push branches + tags, re-add remotes on the LXCs (pct exec git remote set-url on 101/104/106/107/108/109/112/113). Every LXC that pulls/pushes this repo needs its remote re-pointed; I can enumerate them from state.db components table (build_lxc / deploy_target_lxc) at execution time.",
    "R7-SCOPE-INVERSION (rmw3-only, L3 hard-rule scope): when rmw3 rules the scope, the mechanical part is small: (1) if ruling = 'state.db rules table wins only for domains it observes' → amend RULES.md's R7 text (scope carve-out for the rules domain) and update task(R7-SCOPE-INVERSION) status; (2) if ruling = 'migrate the table to match the compressed RULES.md' → write the 14 rows (R1,R4,R5,R6,R7,R8,R9,R10,R10.1,R11,R13,R15 + practice + operational-annex) into state.db via sqlite3 on n02 (R1c: state.db is a permitted host path), then re-run vsf-q rules to show the corrected set and re-verify no parser regressed. Either way the deliverable is one commit + one state.db update with before/after counts.",
    "TASK-STATUS-VOCABULARY-NORMALISE step 3 (mine, once rmw3 rules the ambiguous set): the census+proposal (b6a1859) is still current as the base — re-measured 2026-08-11: the FILE changed under it (355 blocks now, 0 unreadable) but the 32-value vocabulary and the 8-token proposal + mapping table are unchanged and still complete. What step 3 needs: (a) rmw3's ruling on the 6 ambiguous + 3 same-family (§4 of my census report), (b) then I apply the mapping mechanically across TASKS.vsm (status TOKEN ONLY, per dispatch guardrail), declare the closed vocabulary in scripts/tasks_validate.py, and re-run the census for before/after counts. No re-measure needed beyond the one above; nothing new from me is required to start the instant the ruling lands.",
    "git gc --prune=now (rmw3-only): when rmw3 runs it on the repo, I would (1) confirm the orphan object is gone — git cat-file -e on the pre-rollback NATS-token blob must fail, and pickaxe grep for the token across all refs must return 0 hits; (2) confirm the working tree and all branches are unaffected (git fsck clean, git log identical); (3) close the item in the catalog/TASKS with the verification output. Nothing for me to pre-build — it is a single rmw3 command plus my post-verification.",
  ],
}
